mes it's almost always cost  overruns and time overruns and what Webb  did to avoid that was when Kennedy  originally said we want to put a man on  the moon  1968 I think it was and Webb said no it  has to be by the end of the decade  because he wanted to build in that time  and when they originally put their cost  estimate on he knew that they were prone  to kind of the overconfidence and so he  I think he doubled yeah he roughly  doubled yes double the estimate and so  they came pretty close  so they actually met the the hopeful  budget what talk about a recent example  of a big government project that is just  a total screwup a total quagmire let's  not even talk about foreign policy  because we could be here all time but I  mean what what's a good example of  people have asked us what is a big  success in in recent history and it is  difficult to look at large government  undertaking it's easier to thing look at  things that didn't go through  immigration reform 2006 failed to become  reality No Child Left Behind in 2001  clearly hasn't been a success you  wouldn't necessarily describe it as a  total fiasco but clearly didn't deliver  the hope for results I think that the  foreign policy issues the Katrina is  sort of a poster child  Boston's Big Dig is the largest  infrastructure project in American  history  horrible cost over what if what if it is  that the point of these projects is not  act you know and this is kind of a  public choice or in a cult reading of  government activity the point of the Big  Dig or the point of No Child Left Behind  isn't to actually improve education it  is to spend money it's to come up with a  plausible pretext to just employ a lot  of people and spend more and more gobs  of money I think both sides make the  mistake of they have what we call  Tolstoy syndrome or confirmation bias  there they don't let diverse viewpoints  and they don't like criticism come in  and they basically have this sort of  tunnel vision and what would ends up  happening there is you have a bad idea  and you're never going to be successful  with the idea is bad in the first place  and I think you know one of the things  that we point out in the book that it's  it's both you know the left and the  right that that make those mistakes  because we've also seen a lot of  failures in recent years of market  oriented small government downsizing  initiatives that failed and then my  ideas may have been great but their  execution was poor  so talk a little bit about what goes  into the proper planning and execution  of a good idea of a big project that  gets them well part of it is I mean the  idea has to be good one of the things we  do in the book which sort of violates  the rule of writing about government  undertakings is that we look at both the  policy development side of things and  the implementation bureaucratic side of  things generally you're right about  policy and politics or you're right  about public management and we looked at  the journey to success the journey  starting with an idea and going all the  way through results as being a  continuous journaling journey and when  you take those two apart artificially  you'll lose a lot of insights that you  gained by looking at them as a  continuous process because a lot of the  failures that we see in implementation  have their roots in poor legislative  design I think California electricity  reform in the late 90s is a great  example of that it cost governor Gray  Davis the governorship in his in essence  and yet it had been passed unanimously  by the legislature and signed into law  by Governor Pete Wilson and yet Davis  was the guy who when this this plan blew  up and it wasn't really execute  implementation failures it was really  the design failures that then expressed  themselves during the implementation  phase so in that case you need a  workable design that's going to stand up  in the real world and oftentimes we do  these designs just to get them through  the legislatures and they don't work in  the real world that's a big problem you  need to make sure when you're going  through Congress or a state legislature  that the idea doesn't get so distorted  that it's not going to work you need to  have you know a good launch for this and  another thing that we found was most of  these successful initiatives whether  they were small government initiatives  or big government initiatives were  characterized by what we call the  bridger as someone in the bureaucracy  who is really good at bridging the  public and that I mean the politicians  the political side and the bureaucratic  side and essentially they knew the  public sector terrain which is so  different than the private sector and  they can navigate that terrain if you  don't have someone like that it's very  very difficult to be sitting with us  what's a good success story one of the  things we point to is in  than they instituted congestion pricing  which basically means you charge people  to drive in downtown London during  during work days and the thing that we  found interesting about that was that it  was implemented this is a market  oriented reform in essence it's a  difficult one politically to sell but  they had their socialist mayor  environmental reasons use these market  oriented approach that you know Milton  Friedman and other sort of economists  would would would say yes that makes  sense to allocate resources more  efficiently  Bob Poole at The Reason Foundation has  been advocating this sort of thing for a  long time another success in a similar  vein was acid rain reductions from the  1980s which used a cap-and-trade model  and it was a accommodate it was  developed by an economist but it it  again uses market mechanisms to take on  environmental concerns and the  interesting thing I find about kind of  both the California electricity was  called electricity deregulation Road and  was based on this market approach of  choice and competition is going to drive  electricity prices down  but if you design that poorly you can  get into enormous difficulty which  California did which did I mean they  limited the market the deregulation  produced a document that was a foot high  right and in the same way the  cap-and-trade that was very successful  in the 1980s doesn't mean that something  else that's called cap-and-trade is  going to be successful it's all in those  design details that if those aren't  right if the markets aren't shaped  correctly or the incentives aren't  aligned you can have massive problem how  important is it to make sure that you're  that government is only doing certain  types of things because this is you know  in a libertarian organization when it  you know it might even be that the  government could do certain things  efficiently but it shouldn't do them but  then typically government takes on too  much stuff well you know there's  actually two different ways of looking  at that the first one is simply from the  standpoint of non non take Audiology out  of it completely but what we heard from  a lot of the senior executives in  government that we interviewed and that  we surveyed was that they actually  believe that when government is trying  to do too many things then there's a  lack of focus on getting these big  things right and  and so you know what we talked about in  one of our traps is we call the  complacency trap essentially and that's  when we have a lot of programs and  systems and agencies that have built up  over time and that we continue to  essentially do those we haven't really  ated them we haven't looked at them and  we spend a lot of time and energy  managing those in a lot of managerial  talent and that then distracts from  getting some of the more important  things done that we think so take away  ideology completely you have some real  operational problems from failing to  reexamine government choosing wisely is  a critical step and so I think that it's  it's essential that we do make good  choice about what we ask government to  do and I also think that understanding  how government works which this book  looks at is it can only enhance our  decision-making in terms of making those  choices about what we ask government to  do I want to thank Bill Eggers and John  O'Leary authors of the great new book if  we can put a man on the moon getting big  things done in government first talking  to Reason TV today I'm Nicholas
 

